#sky130A - a starter design-rule deck for GDS Viewer.
#
#NOT A SIGNOFF DECK, AND NOT AFFILIATED WITH SKYWATER. This is a hand-transcribed subset for catching
#obvious mistakes while looking at a layout. The foundry signs off against Calibre, not against this.
#
#Layer numbers are from sky130/layers.py in OtherResources/Sky130/skywater130-main.zip, which carries all
#432 pairs. Rule values are transcribed from the public rule documentation at
#https://skywater-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/main/rules/periphery.html
#
#TWO THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TRUSTING A NUMBER HERE
#
#The two documentation pages disagree about rule numbering. rules/summary.html calls the diffusion rules
#diff.1, diff.2 and the metal rules metal1.1, metal1.2; rules/periphery.html calls the same layer difftap
#and numbers poly.2 as a spacing where the summary numbers it as a width. This file follows periphery.html,
#the detailed page. The identifier is a label and nothing else - the checker keys on it for the marker text
#and never for the check - so a wrong id misnames a violation but cannot change what was measured.
#
#Values are DATABASE UNITS, which for sky130 is nanometers: the micron figure in the documentation times
#1000. Areas are square nanometers, so 0.265 um2 is 265000. Nothing is scaled and nothing is rounded.

#Drawn layers ***********************************************************************

layer nwell  64/20
layer diff   65/20
layer tap    65/44
layer poly   66/20
layer licon  66/44
layer nsdm   93/44
layer psdm   94/20
layer npc    95/20
layer li1    67/20
layer mcon   67/44
layer met1   68/20
layer via    68/44
layer met2   69/20
layer via2   69/44
layer met3   70/20
layer via3   70/44
layer met4   71/20
layer via4   71/44
layer met5   72/20
layer hvtp   78/44
layer lvtn  125/44

#Derived layers *********************************************************************
#
#Not optional, and not an advanced feature. difftap.8 is an enclosure of P+ diffusion, which is a layer
#nobody draws, and poly.4 is a spacing for poly ON FIELD - poly with the gate taken out of it.
#
#Three of these - gate, ndiff, ptap - are read by no rule below, because the rules that would have read them
#are in the "deliberately NOT here" list at the foot of this file. They are kept because they are correct
#and because a rule may arrive for them. Nothing is spent meanwhile: a derivation no rule reaches is never
#computed. fieldpoly IS read now, by poly.4, which the edge engine made expressible.

derive gate      = poly and diff
derive fieldpoly = poly not diff
derive pdiff     = diff and psdm
derive ndiff     = diff and nsdm
derive ptap      = tap and psdm
derive ntap      = tap and nsdm

#Rules ******************************************************************************
#
#id           check      operands        value   description

#N-well
rule nwell.1    width      nwell             840   "N-well width"
rule nwell.2    space      nwell            1270   "N-well spacing"

#Diffusion and tap
rule difftap.1  width      diff              150   "Diffusion width"
rule difftap.3  width      tap               150   "Tap width"
rule difftap.4  space      tap               130   "Tap spacing"
rule difftap.8  enclosure  pdiff  nwell      180   "N-well enclosure of p+ diffusion"
rule difftap.10 enclosure  ntap   nwell      180   "N-well enclosure of n+ tap"

#Implants
rule nsdm.1     width      nsdm              380   "N+ implant width"
rule nsdm.2     space      nsdm   diff       130   "N+ implant spacing to diffusion"

#Poly
rule poly.1a    width      poly              150   "Poly width"
rule poly.2     space      poly              210   "Poly spacing"

#And the one this deck could not express until there was an edge engine.
#
#"Spacing of poly on field to diff, PARALLEL EDGES ONLY" - and the qualifier is the whole rule. Measured
#any way that counts corners, every corner approach in a cell is a violation and the real ones are buried
#among them. `parallel` puts the rule on the edge engine, which asks which edges actually run alongside
#each other; a pair whose spans do not overlap when projected is not a pair.
#
#On fieldpoly rather than poly, which is the other half of the rule: poly with the gate taken out of it.
rule poly.4     space      fieldpoly          75   parallel  "Poly on field to diff, parallel edges only"

#Nitride poly cut
rule npc.1      width      npc               270   "Npc width"
rule npc.2      space      npc                90   "Npc spacing"

#Local interconnect and contacts
rule licon.1    width      licon             170   "Licon width"
rule li1.1      width      li1               170   "Li1 width"
rule li1.2      space      li1               170   "Li1 spacing"
rule mcon.1     width      mcon              170   "Mcon width"

#Metal stack
rule met1.1     width      met1              140   "Met1 width"
rule met1.2     space      met1              140   "Met1 spacing"
rule via.1      width      via               150   "Via width"
rule met2.1     width      met2              140   "Met2 width"
rule met2.2     space      met2              140   "Met2 spacing"
rule via2.1     width      via2              280   "Via2 width"
rule met3.1     width      met3              360   "Met3 width"

#Implant marker areas. holearea is a rule on the holes in a merged layer rather than on the layer, which
#is a distinction the documentation makes explicitly and which MergeByLayer already hands back separately.
rule hvtp.5     area       hvtp           265000   "Hvtp min area"
rule hvtp.6     holearea   hvtp           265000   "Hvtp min hole area"
rule lvtn.13    area       lvtn           265000   "Lvtn min area"
rule lvtn.14    holearea   lvtn           265000   "Lvtn min hole area"

#Every coordinate against the manufacturing grid, which on sky130 is 5nm.
#
#The value is stated rather than read back off the file, and that is not an oversight. Grid.Of recovers the
#coarsest grid a library's coordinates all sit on - their greatest common divisor - which is exactly right
#for snapping and circular for checking: one coordinate at 3nm among a file of multiples of 5 drags that
#divisor to 1, and nothing is ever off a grid of 1. The stray coordinate defines away the grid meant to
#catch it. So the grid is PDK data here, like every other number in this file.
rule grid.1     offgrid    *                   5   "Coordinate off the 5nm manufacturing grid"

#Rules deliberately NOT here *********************************************************
#
#These are real sky130 rules that the size-and-boolean engine cannot express, listed so that their absence
#is a decision on the record rather than an oversight. A deck that quietly approximated them would report
#a clean cell that is not clean, which is worse than reporting that it did not look.
#
#poly.2   as written excludes certain adjacent poly pairs. What is checked above is the unqualified
#         spacing, so it can over-report where the exclusion would have applied.
#difftap.2 "Diffusion spacing" 0.340. Taken out after it fired on a signed-off sky130 standard cell, and
#         worth the longest note here because the fault was not where it looked.
#
#         The geometry is real: a 295nm gap between two diffusions, and KLayout's own engine finds the same
#         one on the same cell - so this is not a case of two tools disagreeing, and there is a test that
#         pins them agreeing. What is wrong is the rule as transcribed. sky130's difftap.2 is about two
#         diffusions of the SAME type; the gap in that cell is between n-type and p-type across a well
#         boundary, which a different rule governs at a different distance. Written unqualified it reports
#         the ordinary structure of every standard cell ever drawn.
#
#         Distinguishing the two needs the implants: n+ diffusion is `diff and nsdm`, p+ is `diff and psdm`,
#         and both derivations are already in this file. What is missing is the rule's own qualification -
#         same-type spacing is not "the spacing of a layer to itself" once the layer has been split in two,
#         it is two separate checks plus a third across the boundary. That is transcription work against the
#         rule manual rather than anything the engine cannot do, and it is not done.
#
#poly.7   "Extension of diff beyond poly (min drain)" 0.250, and
#poly.8   "Extension of poly beyond diffusion (endcap)" 0.130. Both are DIRECTIONAL, and both were in this
#         deck until it was run against the bundled transistor. Measured in every direction - which is all
#         a region-based check can do - poly.8 reports the two SIDES of the channel, where the endcap it
#         means is the two ENDS. Every violation the pair produced was the axis the rule does not care
#         about, and not one was the axis it does. `extension` is refused at the door for the same reason.
#
#The parser is expected to REFUSE an unsupported check by name rather than skip it quietly.

#Why there is no notch rule here ****************************************************
#
#sky130 states one, x.6: "all intra-layer separation checks will include a notch check". It carries no
#value, because it is not a separate measurement - it says every spacing limit already written applies
#inside a shape as well as between two.
#
#Every `space` rule above already does that. A closing fills an internal gap and an external one alike, so
#x.6 is satisfied without a line of its own. The `notch` check exists for a deck that wants the two apart,
#giving an internal gap a different limit from an external one; sky130 does not, so nothing here uses it.
